<h2>How Often Does This Wiki Get It Right — Measuring Search and Graph Traversal</h2>
<p>This wiki introduces itself as a "RAG-ready knowledge source," opens a <a href="#!kgs-ai-layer">machine-readable layer</a> to AI, and ships its engine as a <a href="#!kgs-plugin">plugin</a>. Claims like that need numbers behind them. This page measures how often the current site search and the related-pages block get it right, and gives what works and what does not the same weight.</p>

<h3>Summary</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Search is strong on words and mostly finds sentences too.</strong> One or two words, or a few content words, put the right page first almost every time. Asked as a sentence, the right page lands in the top eight 93.8% of the time in Korean and 75% in English and Japanese (on queries frozen in advance). No query came back empty.</li>
    <li><strong>The related block rarely leads nowhere.</strong> Of 199 edges, 4 (2.0%) point somewhere unrelated. But only a third of the links authors wrote into the text also appear in the related block, and only a seventh of the links that cross folders.</li>
    <li><strong>Three weak spots:</strong> conjugated Korean verbs, readers and pages using different words, and connections that cross folders.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why measure</h3>
<p>On 23 September Anthropic widened Claude Marketplace into a catalog of about 2,000 connectors, plugins, products and implementation partners. It had launched on 6 March with six partners; enterprise customers can now spend existing commitments on third-party tools and receive a single invoice. Plugins have become products on a shelf.</p>
<p>The same day, Karpathy's LLM Wiki gist (the pattern in which an LLM builds and queries a markdown wiki directly, without a vector database) drew a comment asking whether anyone had benchmarked the claims about why it works, and that week implementers started posting numbers. This wiki implements that pattern and ships its engine as a plugin. A tool handed to others needs its "it works" turned into a number.</p>

<h3>How it was measured</h3>
<p><strong>Search ran the code the screen uses.</strong> A headless browser opened this wiki's <code>index.html</code>, waited for the knowledge index and the full-text body index, then called the same <code>searchDocs()</code> the search box calls. Each of the Korean, English and Japanese screens was measured separately.</p>
<p><strong>There are two query sets.</strong> Each query lists the page that answers it as the gold answer; where two pages qualify, hitting either counts.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>48 frozen queries</strong>: 32 sentences, 8 one-or-two-word queries and 8 questions that need two pages. They were written and committed before the current search engine was built, so the engine could not be fitted to them. They supply this page's headline numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>107 tuning queries</strong>: one or two words, content words without particles, sentences and two-page questions. They were frozen before any results were seen, but their failures were studied while the engine was refined, so their scores may run high. They appear for reference only.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both sets were written by someone who knows the pages, so their vocabulary sits closer to the text than a real reader's would. This page quotes query sentences verbatim, so it was left out of both the search pool and the graph counts.</p>
<p><strong>The graph was judged against lines authors drew.</strong> The "Related" block under each page (the same <code>related</code> an AI follows in <code>knowledge-graph.json</code>) is computed only from <a href="#!kgs-edges">the rarity of shared concepts</a>. Links authors wrote into the text and typed relations people declared play no part in it, so they make an independent yardstick. Edges backed by neither were judged one by one, by the AI that wrote this page and not by a person.</p>
<p>The harness, query sets and judgments are in the repository under <code>tools/eval/</code> (<code>retrieval.mjs</code>, <code>retrieval-set-fresh.json</code> and <code>retrieval-set.json</code>, <code>edge-audit.json</code>). Re-running it gives the same numbers; changing a judgment gives different ones.</p>

<h3>How search finds pages</h3>
<p>The query is cut into pieces at spaces. Punctuation is stripped from each piece, and for Korean the forms left after cutting common endings and particles are searched too: a Korean question form of "connect" matches if its trimmed stem does, and "code" with a Korean particle glued on is searched as "code." English adds a stem with suffixes like -ing, -ation and -s removed. Japanese has no spaces, so only runs of katakana, kanji and Latin letters are kept, which cuts a sentence into words. Question words such as "how" are dropped. Endings are cut from a list, not by a morphological analyzer, so some forms slip through.</p>
<p>Search then runs in three passes and stops at the first one that returns something.</p>

