<p>Word and content-word queries all landed in the top three in both sets. The difference is in sentences: Korean sentences reach 93.8% on the frozen set and 65% on the tuning set. On the tuning set, 6 of the 20 Korean sentences went to pass 2 in the diagram, and 5 of those missed; that is the verb problem below. With so few queries, one query moves a cell by 5 points or more.</p>

<h3>Where search goes wrong</h3>
<p><strong>Korean verbs.</strong> Pass 2 has the lowest hit rate in the diagram (4 of 9). The dead piece it throws away is usually the verb that carries the question's meaning. Long conjugations, such as the Korean for "get loaded?" or "be trained?", do not shrink to the form the page uses, so they match nowhere. Once the verb is dropped, only common nouns remain, like "wiki" (41 pages), "connect" (41) and "claude" (59), and the answer sinks among them. For a Korean question asking when Claude Code loads skills, the right page came 19th.</p>
<p><strong>Different words.</strong> The page that answers a Korean question about running Claude Code "on a company server" never uses the word for "company" and uses "self-hosting" six times. Every piece appears somewhere, so pass 1 returns results, but the answer is not among them. The same question in Japanese missed the same way. All 18 Japanese sentences got results in pass 1, yet 5 had the answer outside the top eight.</p>
<p><strong>A neighbour that matches more pieces.</strong> For a question about how many stages Second Brain has, the per-level pages ranked above the introduction because they hold more of the query's pieces. The rule that favours pages matching more pieces worked backwards here.</p>

<h3>How right the related block is</h3>
<p>Edges in the related block are decided by the concepts two pages share. Inside one folder, two shared concepts connect them, or one rare concept that appears in three pages or fewer. Across folders it takes three shared concepts, or two where one is rare. A page with fewer than two concept neighbours is topped up with pages from its own folder, except in dated news folders. The full rule is in <a href="#!kgs-edges">How Documents Connect</a>.</p>
