<p>The judging standard: "related" if someone who read the starting page would want the other next; "weak" if the topics overlap but it is not a natural next read; "unrelated" if one common concept or a homonym is all that joins them and following it goes nowhere. Two of the four unrelated edges are one pair, in both directions, between the Skills introduction and the security and roadmap page of the DOUZONE proposal; one of its two shared concepts, "reuse," is rare, so it clears the cross-folder bar. The other two are common-concept edges into the usage article. The other World-crossing pair is the "knowledge management" lineage between an LLM Wiki article and the DOUZONE knowledge platform proposal, which reflects a real relationship.</p>
<p>Recall is the weak side. Of the links authors wrote as "see also this," a third also sit in the related block at one hop, and only a seventh of those that cross folders. That is the price of a high bar across folders. <a href="#!wr-knowledge-vault">The reflection on knowledge stores</a> links to the Second Brain page on levels 3 to 5 in its text, but the two pages share only two concepts that are not rare ("RAG" and "second brain"), so the related block leaves it out. The bar sees what two pages share, not whether the link between them is good. Three pages that sit alone in their folders (the im-not-ai skill, the dews-sourcegraph skill and the notification engine proposal) have no neighbours to fill from, so their related block is empty.</p>

<h3>Joining the two</h3>
<p>Questions whose answer spans two pages joined search and traversal: are both pages in search's top three, and are they once the related blocks of those three pages are added?</p>
