<p>On the tuning set, the related block often supplied the page search had missed, lifting 25% to 75%. On the frozen set, for the 2 questions where search found only one page, the related block did not hold the other, so one hop added nothing. There is a limit here too. Of the 20 pairs in both sets, 18 sit inside one folder, so these questions cannot say how much the related block helps with pairs across folders. Going by the recall figures above, that is probably where it is weak.</p>

<h3>Why these numbers are not set beside other implementations'</h3>
<p>LLM Wiki implementers have posted numbers too. One measured precision over 1,536 queries and reported 0.3086 on its fast tier; another reported 94.0% on the long-term memory benchmark LongMemEval against 71.2% for the comparison. This page does not set its figures beside theirs. They measured systems that store and recall memory on standard benchmarks; this page measured a published wiki's site search and related blocks on its own queries. The targets differ, and their figures are all self-reported. The aim here is not to make numbers look big but to publish the queries and judgments so anyone can measure the same way.</p>

<h3>What to look at next</h3>
<p>The numbers point two ways. In search: reducing Korean verbs to the forms pages use, and a synonym list or meaning-based search that connects "company server" to "self-hosting." In the graph: declaring as typed relations the good cross-folder links the bar misses. Either way, fresh queries get frozen before the change, and the same tool measures again.</p>

<h3>Measurement conditions and fact-check note</h3>
<p class="scn-sub">Measured on 25 September 2026 against the wiki at commit <code>8907dbf</code>: 71 of 72 indexed pages and 73 of 74 searchable pages, this page excluded. Search was run twice and gave identical numbers. The 48 frozen queries were fixed in a separate commit (<code>ac66d57</code>) before the current search engine was built; the 107 tuning queries were fixed before any results were seen, but their failures informed the engine's refinement. Both sets were written by someone who knows the pages, so scores may run higher than on real reader queries. In cells of 8 to 20 queries, one query moves the figure by 5 to 12.5 points. Judgments on unbacked edges were made by the AI that wrote this page, not by a person. The Claude Marketplace expansion (23 September, about 2,000 items, launched 6 March with six partners, purchases against existing commitments on a single invoice) was cross-checked with <a href="https://venturebeat.com/technology/anthropic-launches-claude-marketplace-giving-enterprises-access-to-claude" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VentureBeat</a>, <a href="https://thenextweb.com/news/anthropic-marketplace-claude-enterprise-software" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Next Web</a> and <a href="https://claude.com/platform/marketplace" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the official page</a>. The question on the LLM Wiki gist and the implementers' figures were confirmed in <a href="https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the gist's own comments</a>; all figures are self-reported and no independent reproduction was found. The implementers are private individuals and are not named.</p>
